1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,160
Quick question.

2
00:00:01,160 --> 00:00:03,980
How many subscriptions are you paying for right now?

3
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:06,180
Whatever number you guessed... it's higher.

4
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:07,220
It's always higher.

5
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,040
Right now, money is quietly leaving your account every month —

6
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:11,740
for stuff you forgot you own.

7
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,440
An app. A "free" trial that stopped being free.

8
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:15,920
A show you watched once.

9
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,620
Your free trial of owning things just expired.

10
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:20,240
You don't buy things anymore.

11
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:23,620
You rent your whole life. One payment at a time.

12
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:26,660
Now — this is not a video about how subscriptions are evil

13
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:28,920
and you should throw your phone in a lake.

14
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,340
Some are genuinely useful. Calm down.

15
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,060
This is about something weirder.

16
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,340
We used to own things.

17
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,440
You paid once, the thing was yours, the end.

18
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:38,380
A beautiful, boring transaction.

19
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:39,800
A subscription is different.

20
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,200
You don't buy the thing. You rent access to it.

21
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,140
Pay every month, the screen turns on.

22
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:46,920
Miss a payment, and your own device does this —

23
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:48,060
a little padlock appears,

24
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:51,340
and it looks at you like a disappointed parent.

25
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,220
So here's the real question.

26
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:56,420
How did almost everything — your apps, your shows, your software,

27
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:58,280
even buttons inside your car —

28
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:01,820
quietly turn into stuff you rent instead of stuff you own?

29
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:04,340
It started quietly, with software.

30
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,920
The program you used to buy once, in a box, became a monthly plan.

31
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:08,660
Same program.

32
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,760
Now it just... holds itself hostage.

33
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:12,820
Stop paying, and it greys out, like,

34
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:14,540
"pay me, or the document gets it."

35
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,360
Then your screens joined the party.

36
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:17,780
Music, movies, shows —

37
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,800
you don't own a single song or film.

38
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,220
You rent a giant library,

39
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:24,760
and the second you stop paying, the whole thing vanishes.

40
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:26,180
Poof. POV: you own nothing.

41
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:28,980
And it's never one subscription. It's five.

42
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:31,960
One for shows, one for movies, one for the other shows,

43
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:35,280
one for music, and one for the app that organizes your other apps.

44
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,780
Each one is small.

45
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,820
Together? Bigger than the old cable bill everyone was running from.

46
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,880
We escaped one dungeon and built five smaller ones.

47
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:44,740
And it didn't stop at screens.

48
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:48,260
Some carmakers have tried putting heated seats —

49
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:50,840
seats already sitting in the car you bought —

50
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:52,280
behind a monthly fee.

51
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:55,880
Your free trial of a warm bottom has expired.

52
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,820
So why did everyone switch to this? Simple.

53
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,860
Follow the money. It's always the money.

54
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:02,440
Imagine you sell a coffee machine.

55
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:05,380
You make one sale, you say thank you, and then you wait —

56
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:07,820
maybe years — hoping that person ever comes back.

57
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:10,820
Now imagine instead they pay you a little every month.

58
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:13,060
Automatically. Possibly forever.

59
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:14,720
Even on the months they don't touch the thing.

60
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,680
Which business would you rather own?

61
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:17,980
Yeah. Me too.

62
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:20,380
A customer who buys once is worth a little.

63
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:22,800
A customer who pays every month is worth a lot —

64
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,820
because that payment just keeps coming.

65
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:26,320
Like rain. Or relatives.

66
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:29,540
This is the magic word behind everything now: recurring.

67
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:31,360
Recurring money is predictable.

68
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:34,740
A company can plan its entire future around your forgetfulness.

69
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:36,720
So the goal quietly changed.

70
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,300
It used to be: make something good enough that you buy it.

71
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:43,780
Now it can also be: make something sticky enough that you never quite leave.

72
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:47,000
Less "great product." More "beautiful trap."

73
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,660
Here's the genius part.

74
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,600
To get you in, they make it feel free.

75
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:53,140
"Start your free trial!" No charge today!

76
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:56,240
Just pop in your card — you know, for no reason at all.

77
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:58,840
Totally normal. Strangers love holding your card.

78
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:02,260
And the trial is real. It really is free. For seven days.

79
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:05,640
After that, a tiny countdown you can't see hits zero,

80
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,860
and "free" quietly becomes a payment.

81
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,020
And most people don't cancel on day seven. They forget.

82
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:14,360
Not because they're careless — because forgetting is the design.

83
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:17,320
The charge is small, the date is fuzzy, life is loud.

84
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:20,740
So the little payment just... continues. Forever.

85
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:23,240
A ghost, living in your bank account.

86
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:26,360
Rent free. Well — not rent free. That's the whole problem.

87
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:29,940
Open your bank statement right now and you'll probably find one.

88
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:33,200
A mystery charge. Three dollars. Every month.

89
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,600
For a thing you opened once, in a year you cannot name.

90
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:40,840
Your free trial of financial awareness has expired.

91
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:43,300
Okay, you say. I'll just cancel.

92
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:44,460
Oh, sweet child.

93
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:46,520
Here's the strange law of subscriptions:

94
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:48,330
signing up takes ten seconds.

95
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:50,020
Cancelling takes a vision quest.

96
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,200
Getting in is a big, happy, glowing button.

97
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,560
Getting out is hidden somewhere in your phone like a final boss.

98
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,360
You tap "account." Then "settings." Then "manage."

99
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,720
Then "are you sure?" Then "here, have a discount."

100
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:01,920
Then "we'll miss you."

101
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:06,560
Then a phone number that only answers between two and two-fifteen on a Tuesday.

102
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:07,980
This even has a name.

103
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,220
It's been called negative option billing —

104
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,220
a fancy term for one simple trick:

105
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:14,740
you keep paying unless you actively say stop.

106
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:18,260
So all the remembering, deciding, and escaping? Pushed onto you.

107
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:21,660
Minus a thousand aura points, and you didn't even do anything wrong.

108
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:25,300
So a lot of people just... give up. They keep paying.

109
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,500
Not because they want it — because cancelling feels like a part-time job.

110
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,320
With no salary.

111
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:33,960
Which, fittingly, is also not a subscription.

112
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,600
So why is everything a subscription now?

113
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,500
Because companies found something quietly genius.

114
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,620
The most valuable thing they can sell isn't the app, or the show, or the heated seat.

115
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:45,000
It's your forgetting.

116
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,860
The month you meant to cancel, and didn't.

117
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:48,640
Once you see it, you can't unsee it.

118
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:50,400
The product was never the thing.

119
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:52,040
The product is you — staying.

120
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,020
Now, again: subscriptions aren't evil. Some are worth every cent.

121
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,760
The point is just to notice.

122
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:01,260
To know that "small and monthly" is a design, not an accident.

123
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:04,260
So every so often, pick up the phone that's been billing you —

124
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:05,500
and turn it against them.

125
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,640
Open the bank statement. Read the little charges out loud.

126
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:10,840
Ask which ones you'd actually choose again today.

127
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:13,380
Keep the ones you love. Cancel the ghosts.

128
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:16,000
Because in a world where almost everything is rented,

129
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,260
the most powerful move you've got is remembering what you're paying for.

130
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:23,280
And then quietly enjoying the one thing in your life

131
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:24,210
that is still not a monthly subscription.

132
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:28,056
Your salary. For now.

